---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Equation from "../../components/Equation.astro";
import katex from "katex";

function inlineMath(expression: string) {
  return katex.renderToString(expression, {
    displayMode: false,
    output: "htmlAndMathml",
    throwOnError: true,
  });
}
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Anticipation-Based Learning: Intelligence That Knows Its Limits — Shubhojit"
  description="A reflective, mathematical research proposal connecting predictive regulation and cognitive maps to viability-aware learning and safer AI planning."
>
  <article>
    <div class="meta">Research · AI Safety · Reinforcement Learning</div>
    <h1>Anticipation-Based Learning: Intelligence That Knows Its Limits</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      We often understand what a system was missing only after its assumptions
      have failed. Could an agent learn to notice, sooner, when the future it
      expects is no longer one it should trust?
    </p>

    <p>
      Imagine an AI agent managing a software deployment. It has learned to
      ship changes quickly while keeping the service reliable. Then a new
      dependency appears, or a permission changes, or a configuration arrives
      that the agent has never seen. The agent may still be excellent at
      optimizing deployment speed. What it may not know is that its usual
      picture of the world has become unreliable.
    </p>

    <p>
      I keep returning to a quieter question than “How do we make an agent
      optimize better?” We know a great deal about learning objectives, and
      safety constraints are already studied in reinforcement learning. But
      could safety also shape what the agent learns to expect? Could it learn
      not only which futures are likely, but which ones would leave it unable
      to act safely, and when its own picture of the world is too uncertain
      to guide a decision?
    </p>

    <p>
      I’ll call this research direction <strong>Anticipation-Based Learning
      (ABL)</strong>. The name is a proposal, not a result. I’m borrowing the
      word <em>anticipation</em> from questions in neuroscience about how
      organisms prepare for what may come next. That connection is suggestive,
      but it does not prove that the same ideas will help an artificial agent.
    </p>

    <h2>Why borrow the word anticipation?</h2>
    <p>
      An animal does not wait for every need to become an emergency before
      its body responds. In Peter Sterling’s account of
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.physbeh.2011.06.004" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>allostasis</em></a>,
      regulation is predictive: physiological systems prepare for anticipated
      demands, sometimes before a measurable disturbance has arrived.
      Similarly, can AI systems evaluate what an action may demand or put at
      risk before the consequences arrive?
    </p>

    <p>
      This is not the same as saying that an animal consciously simulates a
      detailed future. Nor does physiological regulation tell us what an
      artificial system ought to value. It gives us a biological example of
      control that is shaped by prediction, and a reason to ask whether
      prediction can be organized around preserving safe options.
    </p>

    <p>
      A second connection comes from cognitive maps. The successor
      representation summarizes which states tend to follow from the current
      one under a policy. It compresses experience into a rough map of likely
      futures, rather than requiring a fresh search through every possible
      path. Research on the
      <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6096039/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">successor representation</a>
      and on
      <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9004662/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">predictive representations, replay, and generalization</a>
      explores how such representations may support flexible behavior.
    </p>

    <p>
      Memory, in this picture, is not only a record of what happened. Replay
      can reactivate parts of experience and update a predictive map; in
      computational models, replay can also help connect experiences or
      evaluate what might follow from them. While neuroscience does not
      settle exactly when replay serves memory, learning, or planning, it
      does make this useful possibility harder to ignore: some of the work
      of deciding may happen before the moment of choice.
    </p>

    <p>
      Many artificial agents already predict consequences. Model-based
      reinforcement learning and planning explicitly evaluate possible
      futures. A language model trained for next-token prediction, by itself,
      does not necessarily maintain a calibrated model of a changing
      environment or know when an action could close off safe options; an
      agent built around it may add tools, memory, and planners to do some of
      that work. ABL asks whether safety-relevant futures and the limits of
      prediction should become central things the learning system represents,
      rather than relying only on a separate filter at decision time.
    </p>

    <p>
      We shouldn't mistake a premature conclusion for a finished path. I'm
      not interested in the claim that biology has already settled that
      matter since it feels like a bridge built on paper. I want to stand on
      the actual bridge and test its weight. And to do that we have to
      describe the shape of the problem, the problem an agent faces when it
      cannot see the full state of the world.
    </p>

    <h2>Start with what the agent cannot see</h2>
    <p>
      A deployment agent does not observe the entire system state directly.
      It sees logs, configuration files, test outcomes, and permission
      settings. Some relevant condition may be hidden, delayed, or ambiguous.
      A useful starting point is a partially observable Markov decision
      process, or POMDP:
    </p>

    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{O},T,Z,r,\mathcal{C})`}
      label="A partially observable decision process is defined by states, actions, observations, transition and observation models, reward, and safety constraints."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> The tuple
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{O},T,Z,r,\mathcal{C})`)} />
      names the parts of this POMDP.
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`\mathcal{S}`)} /> is the set of
      hidden world states,
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`\mathcal{A}`)} /> the available
      actions, and <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`\mathcal{O}`)} /> the
      observations the agent can receive.
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`T(s'\mid s,a)`)} /> is the
      probability of moving to state <strong>s′</strong> from state
      <strong>s</strong> after action <strong>a</strong>;
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`Z(o\mid s)`)} /> is the
      probability of observing <strong>o</strong> when the world is in state
      <strong>s</strong>. <strong>r(s, a)</strong> assigns task reward to a
      state-action pair, and
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`\mathcal{C}`)} /> denotes the
      safety constraints. The agent sees observations rather than the hidden
      state itself, so it must reason from incomplete evidence.
    </p>

    <p>
      The agent can summarize that evidence as a belief distribution. Given
      its previous belief, its last action, and a new observation, a Bayesian
      belief update is:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`b_{t+1}(s')=\eta\,Z(o_{t+1}\mid s')\sum_{s\in\mathcal{S}}T(s'\mid s,a_t)b_t(s)`}
      label="Bayesian belief update: predict the next state from the current belief and action, then reweight it by how likely the new observation is."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> <strong>b<sub>t</sub>(s)</strong>
      is the agent’s current probability that the world is in state
      <strong>s</strong>. The sum first predicts each possible next state
      <strong>s′</strong> using the transition model <strong>T</strong>.
      The observation likelihood <strong>Z(o<sub>t+1</sub> | s′)</strong>
      then raises or lowers that state’s plausibility. <strong>η</strong>
      normalizes the probabilities so they add to one. This update depends on
      the model being useful; if the model misses a new failure mode, a
      confident belief can still be wrong.
    </p>

    <p>
      A conventional objective might maximize expected discounted reward:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\pi^*\in\arg\max_{\pi}\ \mathbb{E}_{\pi}\!\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^t r(s_t,a_t)\right]`}
      label="A reward-maximizing policy chooses actions to maximize expected discounted cumulative reward."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> A policy <strong>π</strong> maps
      what the agent knows to an action. The sum adds rewards over time, and
      <strong>γ</strong>, between zero and one, makes later rewards count less.
      The expectation averages across possible trajectories. This is a useful
      objective, but maximizing it does not by itself tell us whether the
      model is trustworthy in a changed environment or whether a rare,
      irreversible failure is acceptable.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is not an argument that reinforcement learning ignores safety.
      Constrained and risk-sensitive methods already address safety in
      important ways. ABL asks a narrower and more structural question: can
      predictive viability become one of the things the agent learns, rather
      than only a rule applied to its actions afterward?
    </p>

    <h2>A map of likely futures is not the whole world</h2>
    <p>
      The successor representation offers a compact way to describe expected
      futures. Under a policy <strong>π</strong>, it records how often each
      state is expected to be visited, with distant visits discounted:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`M^{\pi}(s,s')=\mathbb{E}_{\pi}\!\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^k\mathbf{1}\{s_{t+k}=s'\}\mid s_t=s\right]`}
      label="The successor representation gives expected discounted future occupancy of state s' when starting at state s and following policy pi."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Start in state <strong>s</strong>
      and follow policy <strong>π</strong>. At each future step
      <strong>k</strong>, the indicator is one if the agent is in
      <strong>s′</strong> and zero otherwise. Discounting by
      <strong>γ<sup>k</sup></strong> makes nearer visits count more, and the
      expectation averages over possible paths. The result is not one imagined
      route; it is a predictive summary of where this policy tends to lead.
    </p>

    <p>
      If reward depends on the state, the successor representation lets us
      calculate value by combining expected occupancy with the reward assigned
      to each state:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`V^{\pi}(s)=\sum_{s'\in\mathcal{S}}M^{\pi}(s,s')\,r(s')`}
      label="When reward is a function of state, value is the successor representation weighted by state reward."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> <strong>M<sup>π</sup>(s,s′)</strong>
      says how much discounted future occupancy to expect in each state;
      <strong>r(s′)</strong> says how rewarding that state is. Multiplying
      them and summing gives <strong>V<sup>π</sup>(s)</strong>, the expected
      return from <strong>s</strong> under this policy. When rewards change
      but the transition structure remains suitable, the predictive map can
      sometimes be reused and reweighted instead of learned from scratch.
    </p>

    <p>
      A predictive map tells us what tends to happen. Safety often needs a
      different answer: from here, can the agent still stay inside acceptable
      conditions? A highly likely future is not necessarily a safe one, and
      an average can hide a rare catastrophe. So I would add safety-relevant
      features to the predictive picture, while keeping explicit risk measures
      for the events that matter most:
    </p>
    <p>
      I’ve written more about predictive maps, replay, and compressed futures
      in my essay <a href="/writing/predictive-planning">“Animals Don’t Plan by
      Enumerating the Future.”</a> Here I’m taking that line of thought in a
      different direction: asking whether predictive representations can help
      an agent recognize futures it should avoid.
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\Psi^{\pi}_{\mathrm{safe}}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi}\!\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^k\phi_{\mathrm{safe}}(s_{t+k},a_{t+k})\mid s_t=s\right]`}
      label="A candidate safety-oriented predictive representation accumulates discounted future safety features under policy pi."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> <strong>ϕ<sub>safe</sub></strong>
      extracts a safety-relevant feature from a state and action, such as
      whether a permission is too broad or data integrity is at risk.
      <strong>Ψ<sup>π</sup><sub>safe</sub>(s)</strong> predicts how those
      features accumulate along futures generated by policy <strong>π</strong>.
      The discount factor makes this a convenient summary, not a complete
      measure of danger: discounting can hide distant harm, and an expected
      value can hide rare severe outcomes.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can also define a nonnegative safety cost <strong>c</strong> and its
      expected cumulative value:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`J^{\pi}_{c}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi}\!\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\gamma^t c(s_t,a_t)\mid s_0=s\right],\qquad c(s,a)\geq 0`}
      label="Expected discounted cumulative safety cost under policy pi, where each state-action safety cost is nonnegative."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> <strong>c(s,a)</strong> assigns
      a nonnegative cost to a state-action pair, and
      <strong>J<sub>c</sub><sup>π</sup>(s)</strong> adds those costs over a
      trajectory, discounting later ones. It can compare policies by expected
      safety burden, but it still does not guarantee that no catastrophic
      event occurs. For that, we may need finite-horizon violation
      probabilities, worst-case analysis, or another explicit risk criterion.
    </p>

    <h2>Viability means more than “safe right now”</h2>
    <p>
      Suppose the deployment is currently healthy. A planned action might
      grant a permission that does not cause immediate damage, but makes a
      later data exposure impossible to prevent. The current state is safe;
      the future options are not.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let the safe set contain states where every specified constraint is
      satisfied. For state constraint functions <strong>g<sub>j</sub></strong>,
      we can write:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}=\{s\in\mathcal{S}:g_j(s)\leq 0\ \text{for every }j=1,\ldots,m\}`}
      label="The safe set consists of states satisfying every specified constraint."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Each constraint
      <strong>g<sub>j</sub>(s) ≤ 0</strong> marks a condition we have chosen
      to regard as acceptable. The safe set is the intersection of all these
      conditions. The functions do not discover what ought to count as safe;
      people and institutions still have to specify and justify them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before asking what is achievable, we need to say which policies the
      agent is allowed to consider. In a partially observed problem, a policy
      acts on the information available so far, not on the hidden state. Let
      <strong>h<sub>t</sub>=(o<sub>1:t</sub>,a<sub>1:t−1</sub>)</strong>
      denote that observation-action history, and let
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ext}}(h_t)`)} /> be the actions allowed
      by externally specified rules, such as a permission boundary or
      human-authorization requirement:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}}=\left\{\pi:\pi(a_t\mid h_t)=0\ \text{whenever }a_t\notin\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ext}}(h_t)\right\}`}
      label="The admissible policy set contains history-dependent policies that assign zero probability to actions forbidden by external rules."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> An admissible policy
      <strong>π</strong> can choose actions using the history
      <strong>h<sub>t</sub></strong>, but it must assign zero probability to
      any action outside the externally allowed set
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ext}}(h_t)`)} />. The restrictions are
      specified independently of the learned viability score. This avoids
      defining “admissible” as “whatever the learned model currently thinks
      is safe,” which would make the safety test circular.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now look ahead a fixed number of steps. Starting from a belief
      <strong>b</strong> over hidden states, define the best achievable
      probability of remaining in the safe set under the modeled dynamics:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`V_H^*(b)=\sup_{\pi\in\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}}}P_{T,Z,\pi}\!\left(s_{t:t+H}\subseteq\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}\mid b_t=b\right)`}
      label="Finite-horizon viability from belief b is the highest probability of remaining safe over H steps among externally admissible policies, under transition and observation models T and Z."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Begin with uncertainty about the
      current state encoded in belief <strong>b</strong>, then consider each
      policy in the externally defined set <strong>Π<sub>adm</sub></strong>.
      The probability is over hidden states, observations, and stochastic
      transitions generated by <strong>T</strong> and <strong>Z</strong>.
      The supremum selects the policy with the highest chance that every
      state from now through horizon <strong>H</strong> stays inside
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}`)} />. This is a model-relative target, not
      a guarantee about the real system.
    </p>

    <p>
      With a tolerated failure probability <strong>δ</strong>, the
      finite-horizon viability region is:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\mathcal{K}_{H,\delta}=\{b\in\Delta(\mathcal{S}):V_H^*(b)\geq 1-\delta\}`}
      label="The finite-horizon viability region contains beliefs from which some admissible policy keeps the probability of safety at least one minus delta."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> A belief belongs to
      <strong>𝒦<sub>H,δ</sub></strong> when the best externally admissible policy can
      keep the probability of staying safe at or above
      <strong>1 − δ</strong> for the chosen horizon.
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`\Delta(\mathcal{S})`)} /> is the
      set of probability distributions over states. If
      <strong>δ</strong> is small, the requirement is stricter. This
      separates “nothing bad has happened yet” from “there is still a
      sufficiently safe way forward.”
    </p>

    <p>
      This is the idea behind <em>viability before optimization</em>. It
      doesn’t mean an agent must solve every safety problem before learning
      anything. It means that its learned predictions should help determine
      which futures remain admissible, and its task optimizer should choose
      among those futures rather than treating safety as just another
      preference to trade away.
    </p>

    <h2>Uncertainty belongs in the prediction, too</h2>
    <p>
      A model can be wrong in at least three different ways. The world may
      produce different outcomes from the same state and action; the current
      observation may leave several hidden states plausible; or the learned
      transition model may be uncertain because it has little evidence for
      this context. Those uncertainties call for different responses.
    </p>

    <p>
      For a fixed candidate policy <strong>π</strong>, let
      <strong>q<sub>θ</sub>(s,π)</strong> be the model’s probability of
      remaining safe for the chosen horizon when starting in state
      <strong>s</strong>. A model parameter <strong>θ</strong> might be
      sampled from a posterior or represented by one ensemble member.
      Integrating over the current belief gives that model’s safety
      prediction:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`q_{\theta}(b,\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{s\sim b}\!\left[q_{\theta}(s,\pi)\right],\qquad \bar q(b,\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim p(\theta\mid\mathcal{D})}\!\left[q_{\theta}(b,\pi)\right],\qquad U_{\mathrm{ep}}(b,\pi)=\operatorname{Var}_{\theta\sim p(\theta\mid\mathcal{D})}\!\left[q_{\theta}(b,\pi)\right]`}
      label="Integrate each model's state-conditioned safety probability over belief b, then compute its posterior mean and epistemic variance across model parameters."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> First average a model’s
      state-conditioned safety probability over the current belief
      <strong>b</strong>, which gives <strong>q<sub>θ</sub>(b,π)</strong>.
      Then average that quantity across plausible models to get
      <strong>q̄</strong>, and measure how much the model-specific estimates
      vary to get <strong>U<sub>ep</sub></strong>. An ensemble approximates
      the distribution over <strong>θ</strong>. This variance is uncertainty
      about the model’s safety estimate, not a probability of failure; models
      that share the same blind spot can still agree.
    </p>

    <p>
      The law of total variance makes the roles of belief uncertainty and
      model uncertainty more explicit. For clarity, assume the model
      posterior and current state belief factorize as
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`p(\theta,s\mid\mathcal{D},o_{1:t})\approx p(\theta\mid\mathcal{D})b_t(s)`)} />;
      a fully Bayesian treatment would use their joint distribution. Let
      <strong>Y</strong> be one if the whole future trajectory stays safe
      and zero otherwise:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\operatorname{Var}(Y\mid b,\mathcal{D},\pi)=\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{\theta,s}[q_{\theta}(s,\pi)(1-q_{\theta}(s,\pi))]}_{\text{stochastic outcomes}}+\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\operatorname{Var}_{s\sim b}[q_{\theta}(s,\pi)]}_{\text{state uncertainty in }b}+\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim b}[q_{\theta}(s,\pi)]}_{\text{model uncertainty}}`}
      label="Total predictive variance of the binary safe-trajectory outcome decomposes into stochastic outcomes, variation across hidden states in the current belief, and variation across plausible models."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> The first term is randomness in
      future outcomes even if the current state and model were known. The
      second measures how much the safety probability differs across hidden
      states that the current belief considers plausible. The third measures
      disagreement across plausible model parameters after averaging over
      that belief. The second term is a decision-relevant measure of
      belief-induced variation, not the entropy of the belief itself; a
      belief can be broad over states with similar safety consequences.
      Keeping these terms separate helps avoid treating state ambiguity as
      model ignorance or either one as environmental randomness.
    </p>

    <p>
      In practice, a simulator can estimate each term by sampling a model
      <strong>θ</strong>, a hidden state from <strong>b</strong>, and
      stochastic rollouts under <strong>π</strong>. The estimates still
      depend on whether the ensemble covers plausible failures and whether
      the belief is calibrated. Uncertainty should not mean “the world is
      dangerous”; it means the evidence may not justify a confident action.
      Depending on the stakes, the agent might gather information, choose a
      reversible step, pause, or ask for authorization.
    </p>

    <h2>Put the pieces into a controller</h2>
    <p>
      Given a belief about the current world, a learned transition model,
      safety constraints, and uncertainty estimates, the agent can compare
      candidate action sequences. For a sequence
      <strong>𝐚 = (a<sub>t</sub>, …, a<sub>t+H−1</sub>)</strong>, let the
      expected task return be:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`J_r(\mathbf{a})=\mathbb{E}\!\left[\sum_{k=0}^{H-1}\gamma^k r(s_{t+k},a_{t+k})\mid b_t,\mathbf{a}\right]`}
      label="Expected discounted task return for a candidate action sequence over horizon H, conditioned on the current belief."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> For each candidate sequence,
      simulate possible futures from the current belief <strong>b<sub>t</sub></strong>.
      Add the rewards along each future, discounting later rewards, then
      average across the model’s predicted outcomes. This gives the sequence
      a task-performance score. It is only as trustworthy as the belief and
      model used to generate those outcomes.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can choose the highest-return sequence only when its predicted safety
      probability remains acceptable after accounting for model uncertainty:
    </p>
    <p>
      For this illustration, treat each candidate sequence as a finite-horizon
      policy plan <strong>π<sub>𝐚</sub></strong>. A full partially observed
      planner can instead branch on future observations and replan inside the
      horizon.
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\begin{aligned}\max_{\mathbf{a}}\quad &J_r(\mathbf{a})\\\text{subject to}\quad &\bar q(b_t,\pi_{\mathbf{a}})-\kappa\sqrt{U_{\mathrm{ep}}(b_t,\pi_{\mathbf{a}})}\geq 1-\delta.\end{aligned}`}
      label="Choose the highest-return action sequence only when its mean predicted safety probability, reduced by a model-uncertainty margin, meets the required threshold."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Among candidate sequences
      <strong>𝐚</strong>, maximize expected task return
      <strong>J<sub>r</sub></strong>, but accept a sequence only if its
      mean predicted safety probability
      <strong>q̄(b<sub>t</sub>,π<sub>𝐚</sub>)</strong>, reduced by
      <strong>κ</strong> times model disagreement, remains at least
      <strong>1 − δ</strong>. Here <strong>κ</strong> controls how
      conservatively model uncertainty is treated. This is a heuristic
      uncertainty margin, not a formal confidence bound unless the model
      distribution and calibration justify that interpretation. Execute
      only the first action, observe the result, update the belief, and plan
      again.
    </p>

