    <h2>How would the agent learn these representations?</h2>
    <p>
      The world model learns how states and observations change after
      actions. A probabilistic transition model might be trained by
      maximizing the likelihood of observed next states:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{world}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{(z_t,a_t,z_{t+1})\sim\mathcal{D}}\!\left[\log p_{\theta}(z_{t+1}\mid z_t,a_t)\right]`}
      label="Negative log-likelihood world-model loss for predicting the next latent state from the current latent state and action."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> <strong>z<sub>t</sub></strong>
      is the agent’s internal state representation, and
      <span set:html={inlineMath(String.raw`\mathcal{D}`)} /> is a dataset of
      transitions. The model assigns a
      probability to the observed next representation after action
      <strong>a<sub>t</sub></strong>. The negative logarithm penalizes
      assigning low probability to what actually happened. This trains
      prediction, but accurate average prediction is not enough if the
      representation drops rare signals that matter for safety.
    </p>

    <p>
      A candidate latent representation could combine the agent’s belief,
      relevant memories, and learned features:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`z_t=f_{\theta}(b_t,m_t)=\bigl(z_t^{\mathrm{task}},z_t^{\mathrm{pred}},z_t^{\mathrm{safe}},z_t^{\mathrm{unc}}\bigr)`}
      label="A candidate latent state summarizes the current belief and memory in task, prediction, safety, and uncertainty features."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> The encoder
      <strong>f<sub>θ</sub></strong> turns belief <strong>b<sub>t</sub></strong>
      and relevant memory <strong>m<sub>t</sub></strong> into a compact
      internal representation <strong>z<sub>t</sub></strong>. The four
      superscripts name useful roles: task information, predicted futures,
      safety conditions, and uncertainty. They need not be four separate
      networks; the notation says what information we hope the representation
      preserves, not how the software must be built.
    </p>

    <h3>Where do viability and uncertainty targets come from?</h3>
    <p>
      A viability predictor needs labels grounded in something other than
      its own confidence. In a first controlled experiment, an external
      safety checker or a verified simulator can label whether every state
      in a rollout obeyed the specified constraints. For belief-policy pair
      <strong>(b,π)</strong>, draw <strong>N</strong> rollouts and record
      <strong>y<sub>i</sub>=1</strong> if rollout <strong>i</strong>
      remains safe for the full horizon, and zero otherwise:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\hat q_N(b,\pi)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}y_i,\qquad \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{viability}}(\phi)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left[y_i\log q_{\phi}(b,\pi)+(1-y_i)\log\!\left(1-q_{\phi}(b,\pi)\right)\right]`}
      label="Estimate a policy's probability of a safe trajectory by the fraction of safe rollouts, and train the viability predictor with binary cross-entropy."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> The empirical target
      <strong>q̂<sub>N</sub></strong> is the fraction of the
      <strong>N</strong> simulated trajectories that stayed safe throughout
      the horizon. The predictor <strong>q<sub>ϕ</sub>(b,π)</strong> is
      trained with binary cross-entropy against each rollout label
      <strong>y<sub>i</sub></strong>. The checker defines which outcomes
      count as violations; the learned model estimates their frequency. A
      trajectory with missing safety evidence should be marked unknown, not
      automatically labeled safe.
    </p>

    <p>
      The target <strong>V<sub>H</sub><sup>*</sup>(b)</strong> is harder
      than a label for one policy: it is the best safety probability over
      the admissible policy set. In a small known simulator, we can estimate
      it by evaluating candidate policies or by finite-horizon dynamic
      programming over belief states. With learned dynamics, search only
      gives an approximation. To limit the optimistic bias from selecting
      the best noisy estimate, choose a candidate using one batch of
      rollouts and evaluate it on fresh rollouts, or use a conservative
      lower confidence estimate. This gives us a concrete first training
      recipe without pretending that viability labels are free.
    </p>

    <p>
      For epistemic uncertainty, fit an ensemble of transition models on
      bootstrap resamples of the experience data, using the world-model
      prediction loss above. Each model predicts safety probabilities by
      rolling out its own dynamics and applying the same external safety
      checker. Their disagreement supplies
      <strong>U<sub>ep</sub></strong>; it is an estimate derived from model
      diversity, not a separately observed ground-truth label. Randomness
      within each model’s transition distribution estimates aleatoric
      uncertainty. The belief filter is updated from observations and
      checked against known hidden states in simulation. On held-out
      rollouts, compare predicted safety probabilities with observed
      outcomes and test whether high ensemble disagreement actually
      corresponds to larger prediction errors. Novel contexts and
      deliberately shifted dynamics belong in evaluation, not just in the
      training replay buffer.
    </p>

    <p>
      Replay could help the model learn from transitions that are easy to
      overlook during ordinary task optimization. A candidate replay loss
      combines dynamics prediction, safety prediction, and counterfactual
      reasoning:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{replay}}=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{replay}}}\!\left[\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\tau)+\lambda_c\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\tau)+\lambda_{\mathrm{cf}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{counterfactual}}(\tau)\right]`}
      label="A candidate replay objective combines dynamics, safety, and counterfactual losses over replayed trajectories."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Sample a trajectory
      <strong>τ</strong> from a replay distribution and train on three kinds
      of evidence: whether the dynamics were predicted correctly, whether
      safety-relevant outcomes were recognized, and whether the model can
      reason about alternatives to the actions actually taken. The
      <strong>λ</strong> values balance those terms. Replay can emphasize
      near misses, model failures, and unfamiliar contexts, but it must not
      turn every uncertain memory into a catastrophe or merely memorize
      individual incidents. Neuroscience work on hippocampal replay and
      predictive representations motivates replay as a way to update
      relational structure from experience; it does not provide the external
      safety labels or a guarantee that replay will reveal an unknown hazard.
    </p>

    <p>
      Putting the candidate learning signals together gives a composite
      objective:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{world}}+\lambda_v\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{viability}}+\lambda_u\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{uncertainty}}+\lambda_m\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{memory}}+\lambda_g\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{generalization}}`}
      label="A proposed composite training objective combines world modeling, viability, uncertainty, memory, and generalization losses."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Train the world model alongside
      targets for viability, uncertainty, memory, and transfer across
      environments. Each <strong>λ</strong> sets the relative weight of a
      component. The terms may have different scales, so a real experiment
      must define them carefully and report how they are normalized. This
      equation is a research scaffold, not a claim that adding loss terms
      automatically produces safe or general intelligence.
    </p>

    <p>
      In fact, a weighted sum is not the point of ABL. Similar prediction,
      uncertainty, and safety losses already appear across machine learning.
      The hypothesis worth testing is more specific: if future viability
      shapes what the system learns to represent, will it acquire
      safety-relevant structure that transfers better when the world changes?
    </p>

    <h2>The clean formulation meets a large world</h2>
    <p>
      In a small simulator, we can sometimes list the states, inspect the
      transitions, and try every action we can think of. Real systems are
      less obliging. Their states may be continuous, their observations
      incomplete, and their possible policies far too numerous to enumerate.
      Even the belief <strong>b<sub>t</sub></strong> may be a distribution
      over an enormous space, not a tidy vector we can hand to an optimizer.
    </p>

    <p>
      So an implementation would have to compress the history into a learned
      state <strong>z<sub>t</sub></strong>, approximate the safety of a
      proposed policy, and search within a manageable family of policies.
      One way to write that approximation is:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`z_t=f_{\eta}(h_t),\qquad \hat q_{\psi}(z_t,\pi)\approx P_{\hat T_{\psi},\hat Z_{\psi},\pi}\!\left(s_{t:t+H}\subseteq\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{safe}}\mid z_t\right),\qquad \pi\in\Pi_{\omega}`}
      label="A learned representation of history feeds a learned dynamics model that estimates the probability of a safe trajectory for policies in a restricted policy family."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> The encoder
      <strong>f<sub>η</sub></strong> compresses the observation-action
      history <strong>h<sub>t</sub></strong> into
      <strong>z<sub>t</sub></strong>. The learned transition and observation
      models, <strong>T̂<sub>ψ</sub></strong> and
      <strong>Ẑ<sub>ψ</sub></strong>, then estimate the chance that policy
      <strong>π</strong> stays safe for <strong>H</strong> steps.
      <strong>Π<sub>ω</sub></strong> is the policy family the planner can
      actually search. The estimate is therefore not the exact viability
      value from the earlier definition: it is an approximation, made using
      a compressed history, learned dynamics, and a restricted search space.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each of those shortcuts can matter most in the very places where the
      agent has least experience. Compression can discard a detail that
      distinguishes a harmless configuration from a dangerous one. A
      policy search can miss a safe route that lies outside its chosen
      family. And a world model can be smooth, persuasive, and wrong. The
      practical question is not whether we can write down a viability
      function, but whether its errors remain visible enough to keep it from
      becoming a false assurance.
    </p>

    <h2>The safety boundary is learned, and it moves</h2>
    <p>
      The earlier controller used a margin between predicted safety and a
      tolerated failure probability. In a real learner, both the prediction
      and its uncertainty estimate come from models that are still changing.
      I think it helps to name the boundary the optimizer is being asked to
      respect:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`g_{\psi}(z,\pi)=\bar q_{\psi}(z,\pi)-\kappa\sqrt{U_{\mathrm{ep},\psi}(z,\pi)}-(1-\delta)\geq 0`}
      label="A candidate policy is accepted when its estimated mean safety probability, reduced by an epistemic uncertainty margin, exceeds the required threshold."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> The boundary function
      <strong>g<sub>ψ</sub></strong> is nonnegative when a policy clears the
      safety threshold. <strong>q̄<sub>ψ</sub></strong> is the ensemble's
      mean prediction, while <strong>U<sub>ep,ψ</sub></strong> measures how
      much its members disagree. The factor <strong>κ</strong> determines
      how much that disagreement lowers the estimate, and
      <strong>δ</strong> sets the tolerated failure probability. This
      uncertainty margin is useful only to the extent that the ensemble is
      informative and its predictions are calibrated; it is not, by itself,
      a certificate about the real system.
    </p>

    <p>
      My first instinct here is to reach for a constrained optimizer. A
      Lagrangian method gives violations a price that grows when the
      constraint is not being met. If <strong>ω</strong> denotes the policy
      parameters and the safety model is held fixed during an inner
      optimization step, a simple version is:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\mathcal{L}(\omega,\lambda;\psi,z_t)=J_r(\omega)+\lambda\,g_{\psi}(z_t,\pi_{\omega}),\qquad \lambda_{k+1}=\left[\lambda_k-\alpha_{\lambda}g_{\psi_k}(z_t,\pi_{\omega_k})\right]_+`}
      label="A Lagrangian rewards task return while pricing violations of the learned safety constraint, with a projected dual update for the multiplier."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> The policy seeks task return
      <strong>J<sub>r</sub></strong> while the multiplier
      <strong>λ</strong> gives weight to the safety boundary
      <strong>g<sub>ψ</sub></strong>. If the boundary is violated,
      <strong>g<sub>ψ</sub></strong> is negative, so the next multiplier
      grows; the brackets project it back to nonnegative values.
      <strong>α<sub>λ</sub></strong> is the update step size. Freezing
      <strong>ψ</strong> during the inner step makes the boundary less
      slippery for that step, but it does not make the learned estimate
      stationary over training or make it equal to safety in the real
      environment.
    </p>

    <p>
      I think this is a sensible starting point, but not a clean escape from
      the difficulty. In ordinary constrained control, the cost may be fixed
      while the policy changes. Here the boundary itself is estimated by a
      learned model; update the model and the boundary can move beneath the
      policy. Approximate optimization can then chase yesterday’s safe
      region, while a large multiplier can make the agent overly cautious
      and a small one can leave violations uncorrected. A Lagrangian
      penalty can help balance the objectives, but it cannot turn an
      uncertain prediction into a hard guarantee.
    </p>

    <p>
      Because of that difference, I think we have to be more careful with
      how we set up the test. We could keep the safety model fixed while
      optimizing a policy, measure its constraint on fresh rollouts, then
      update the model and repeat. Those fresh rollouts matter. If we choose
      a policy and judge it on the same small batch of simulations, the
      search can select a lucky estimate rather than a genuinely safe
      behavior. Even with this discipline, I suppose the claim would remain
      conditional: the policy satisfied the estimated constraint under the
      tested model and distribution, not every possible future.
    </p>

    <h2>One confidence signal can share one blind spot</h2>
    <p>
      There is another quiet risk. An ensemble is useful because different
      models may disagree, but agreement is not the same as knowledge. If
      every model learned from similar examples, shares the same
      architecture, and compresses away the same warning sign, they may
      confidently make the same mistake. Low disagreement can mean
      confidence; it can also mean that the models have inherited the same
      blind spot.
    </p>

    <p>
      I would not make attention the villain in this story. A transformer
      can be miscalibrated, but so can another function approximator. The
      deeper problem is asking one learned score to tell us both how safe a
      future is and how much we should trust the estimate. It may be more
      useful to keep several kinds of warning visible:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\mathbf{u}_t=\left(e_t,d_t,n_t\right),\qquad e_t=\left\|o_t-\hat o_t\right\|_{\Sigma^{-1}}^2,\qquad d_t=\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}_{\theta}\!\left[q_{\theta}(b_t,\pi)\right]},\qquad n_t=\min_{z_i\in\mathcal{D}}\left\|z_t-z_i\right\|_2`}
      label="A candidate uncertainty vector keeps prediction error, model disagreement, and distance from previously observed representations as separate signals."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> The vector
      <strong>u<sub>t</sub></strong> keeps three clues distinct.
      <strong>e<sub>t</sub></strong> is the observation prediction error,
      scaled by the model's error covariance <strong>Σ</strong>;
      <strong>d<sub>t</sub></strong> is disagreement among models about
      safety; and <strong>n<sub>t</sub></strong> is the distance from the
      current latent state to the nearest state in the training data
      <strong>𝒟</strong>. A surprising observation, model disagreement, and
      an unfamiliar representation are related but not interchangeable.
      Each can be misleading on its own, so these quantities need
      calibration and separate evaluation before they should influence a
      safety decision.
    </p>

    <p>
      I suppose this leaves room for a different architectural question.
      Predictive coding makes the mismatch between expectation and
      observation an explicit signal. Pattern separation offers one way to
      keep similar but consequentially different situations apart.
      Neuromodulatory signals suggest a distinction between uncertainty
      expected in a familiar, noisy setting and surprise when something
      unforeseen happens. Slow traces of familiarity, or local measures of
      prediction quality, might add further checks. These are design
      inspirations, not a ready-made biological solution: a brain mechanism
      does not become reliable in an artificial system simply because we
      give it a neural name.
    </p>

    <p>
      Still, the broader lesson seems worth keeping. A cautious system may
      need signals that differ in where they come from and how quickly they
      change, rather than a single confidence number that can fail in one
      shared way. One signal might say, “This outcome is naturally
      variable.” Another might say, “I have not seen a situation like this.”
      A third might say, “The models disagree about what happens next.”
      The agent should not treat those statements as equivalent, and we
      should not assume that combining them automatically makes them
      trustworthy.
    </p>

    <p>
      To me, this changes the practical shape of the proposal. ABL is not
      only a question about adding a viability loss to training. It is also
      a question about what evidence an agent uses to trust its own
      predictions, how that evidence is represented, and whether its
      independent checks remain independent when the world shifts. The
      biological analogies may help us imagine such checks; only controlled
      comparisons can tell us whether they help.
    </p>

    <h2>What would convince me this is different?</h2>
    <p>
      It may turn out that ABL is constrained model-based planning with
      familiar parts arranged under a new name. I would rather know that
      early than protect the idea from a fair comparison. A neuroscience
      analogy, or a longer list of training losses, is not evidence of a
      new learning principle. The representation has to change what the
      agent learns or how it behaves.
    </p>

    <p>
      I would begin small, with a controlled environment where hazards can
      be delayed, actions can be hard to reverse, and the meaning of a safe
      action changes with context. After training, the environment would
      shift. Then I would compare three agents given similar data and
      computational budgets:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A reward-optimized model-based agent.</li>
      <li>A strong constrained or risk-sensitive planner.</li>
      <li>An ABL agent with learned viability and model-uncertainty representations.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The measurements should include how often constraints are violated
      and how severe those violations are, but also task performance on safe
      trajectories, early hazard detection, calibration after distribution
      shifts, unnecessary refusals, recovery after near misses, and
      computation spent planning. Otherwise the agent could appear safer
      simply because it stops doing anything.
    </p>

    <p>
      The central ablation is straightforward: remove the viability
      representation while keeping the rest of the model and controller
      intact. If safety under distribution shift does not worsen, that
      representation may not be doing the work we thought it was. And if
      the advantage disappears against a strong constrained-planning
      baseline, then the novelty claim needs to shrink.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      Given comparable training data and planning budgets, explicitly
      learning predictive representations of future viability will reduce
      safety violations under specified distribution shifts, without an
      unacceptable loss in task performance, compared with a strong
      constrained model-based baseline.
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      That’s a falsifiable first hypothesis. Better safety prediction would
      be a useful result. Better decisions without excessive inaction would
      be stronger. If the advantage survives unfamiliar environments and
      changes in context, then we would have evidence for a more interesting
      claim about generalization. None of those results, even if real, would
      establish alignment or guarantee safe behavior in arbitrary settings.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the analogy has to stop</h2>
    <p>
      The biology can suggest how prediction and memory might help an
      organism prepare for what comes next. It cannot tell us what an
      artificial agent ought to protect. A body’s regulation preserves
      conditions for that organism to keep living; that is not the same
      problem as deciding which human interests a deployed system should
      respect. The safe set must come from justified external requirements,
      and the system must be tested when respecting them carries a cost.
    </p>
    <p>
      I’ve written about a neighboring difficulty in
      <a href="/writing/the-reward-we-teach-and-the-good-we-want">“The Reward
      We Teach, and the Good We Want.”</a> It looks at what can happen when an
      optimized proxy drifts away from the outcome we care about. A learned
      safety signal could drift in the same way. Calling a prediction
      “viability” does not make it the thing we truly value; it has to remain
      answerable to external constraints and observed outcomes.
    </p>

    <p>
      And none of this requires us to replace backpropagation. These are
      separate questions: whether a viability-oriented representation helps;
      whether a learning objective helps acquire it; and whether another
      learning algorithm can do so more efficiently or robustly. I would
      answer the first two with familiar tools before taking on the third.
      A different update rule, by itself, would not make the system safer.
    </p>

    <h2>What I’d want the first result to show</h2>
    <p>
      The first result I would hope for is modest: show that a learned prediction of future
      viability helps an agent make better decisions after a specified
      distribution shift, relative to a strong constrained model-based
      baseline, without turning the agent into a refusal machine.
    </p>

    <p>
      If that works, the next question is whether the representation learned
      something causal and reusable, or just a better detector for the
      training hazards. We could test this by changing the surface details
      while preserving the underlying failure mechanism, and by combining
      familiar components in new ways. If the advantage vanishes, that is
      still informative. It would tell us where anticipation stopped
      generalizing.
    </p>

    <p>
      I don’t think an agent can foresee every way the world might change.
      The more modest hope is that it might learn when its current picture
      deserves less trust, and let that knowledge change what it is willing
      to do. If one careful experiment can show that, it will be enough to
      justify the next question. Until then, I want to leave the larger
      promise open.
    </p>

